{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531", "title": "Low-Tech Fallbacks", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting low-tech fallbacks to school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "08", "section": "06", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531", "cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/08/05"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Low-Tech Fallbacks\n\n![Low-Tech Fallbacks textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-06-low-tech-fallbacks.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA low-tech fallback is the part of a technology plan that decides what happens when the technology is not available. The AI tool will be down, a family will decline consent, a device will fail, and a network will drop. If the lesson only works when the tool works, then the tool has quietly become mandatory, and the students who cannot use it are simply left out. This section treats the fallback as a requirement of any defensible AI policy memo: a plan in which the AI tool is never the single point of failure for learning.\n\nThis matters because the learning target, not the tool, is what a teacher is accountable for. Persuasive writing is the target; the AI writing coach is one optional path to it. A policy memo that names an equivalent non-AI path keeps every student moving toward the target and answers, in advance, the privacy, consent, and equity objections a reviewer will raise.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA policy memo states: \"All students will use the AI writing coach for the persuasive essay unit.\" Then a family opts out, two students have no reliable device at home, and the vendor's terms turn out to permit retention of student text for training. The memo has no answer for any of these, because it assumed the tool always works and everyone consents. The students without a path are penalized by omission. The question this section answers is how to write the memo so that opting out, losing the device, or refusing consent never costs a student the learning.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible AI policy memo runs the proposed use through four checks and then names a fallback that survives all of them:\n\n- **Privacy.** State what happens to student data. A memo should specify, for example, that the vendor contract bars retention of student text for model training. Silence on data is itself a finding.\n- **Consent.** Assume some families will decline, and give those students a real path. A memo with no opt-out path makes the tool mandatory by omission.\n- **Equity.** Identify who is disadvantaged by the logistics. Students without reliable home devices cannot use a tool assigned as homework, so any homework dependence becomes an access penalty. Keeping AI use in class on school devices removes that penalty.\n- **Learning purpose plus fallback.** Tie the use to the actual target, then provide an equivalent non-AI path that reaches the same target. A printed peer-review protocol and a teacher conference can reach a persuasive-writing target without any tool.\n\nThe organizing idea is single-point-of-failure avoidance: a lesson should degrade gracefully when the technology is gone. The fallback is not a lesser version for \"those kids\"; it is an equivalent route that any student could take. Critical edtech studies supplies the habit of asking who is excluded when a tool is assumed, and [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) keeps the fallback aimed at the same content goal rather than at a watered-down substitute.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare a memo that assumes the tool always works with one that builds in a fallback.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: a low-tech fallback inside the AI policy memo\n\nScenario: a school is drafting an AI policy memo and a teacher must specify what happens when the AI tool is unavailable, the family declines consent, or the device fails. A defensible memo names a low-tech fallback so the AI tool is never the single point of failure for learning.\n\nBefore (memo that assumes the tool always works):\n\n> Policy memo: All students will use the AI writing coach for the persuasive essay unit.\n\nWalk the memo against the four policy checks, then add the fallback:\n\n1. Privacy check. Student essays may contain personal detail; the memo must say data is not retained by the vendor for training. The before memo is silent.\n2. Consent check. Some families will decline. The before memo gives those students no path, which makes the AI tool mandatory by omission.\n3. Equity check. Students without reliable home devices cannot use the tool at home, so any homework dependence is an access penalty.\n4. Learning-purpose plus fallback. The target is persuasive writing, not tool use, so an equivalent non-AI path must exist that reaches the same target.\n\nAfter (annotated memo with a low-tech fallback):\n\n> Policy memo (08.06): The AI writing coach is one option for the persuasive essay unit, not a requirement. Privacy: vendor contract bars retention of student text for model training. Consent: families may opt out; opted-out and no-device students use the same task with a printed peer-review protocol and a teacher conference slot, which reach the same persuasive-writing target. Equity: all AI use happens in class on school devices, never as required homework. Decision: low-tech path is the default fallback so no student's learning depends on the tool being available.\n\nThe after memo is stronger because it makes the AI tool optional, names the fallback that protects the learning target, and answers the privacy, consent, and equity checks a reviewer would raise.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Monday morning: the AI writing coach is down\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your persuasive-essay unit starts today, but the AI writing coach platform is offline and one family opted out on Friday. Your memo said \u2019all students will use the AI writing coach.\u2019 What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Wait for the platform to come back and lose the class period.\",\"next\":\"wait\",\"feedback\":\"The learning target (persuasive writing) does not depend on the tool. Waiting lets the tool be a single point of failure.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the equivalent low-tech path: printed peer-review protocol and a teacher conference slot.\",\"next\":\"fallback\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. The fallback reaches the same target, so no student loses the learning.\"}]},\"wait\":{\"prompt\":\"The platform is still down at the end of the period. The opted-out student also has nothing to do. What does this reveal about the memo?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It is fine; outages are rare.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"The failure already happened. A fallback improvised after exclusion is too late.\"},{\"label\":\"The memo made the tool mandatory by omission and had no failure plan.\",\"next\":\"fallback\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The fix is an equivalent fallback that any student can take, written in advance.\"}]},\"fallback\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague suggests the opted-out and no-device students do a shorter, easier version of the task to keep it simple. Accept it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, a lighter version is fair enough.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"A lesser fallback fixes access on paper but opens a quality gap. The fallback must be equivalent, not reduced.\"},{\"label\":\"No: the printed protocol must reach the same persuasive-writing target as the AI path.\",\"next\":\"equity\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Equivalent, not lesser, is the standard.\"}]},\"equity\":{\"prompt\":\"Where should the required AI use happen so no-device students are not penalized at home?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Assigned as homework so class time is saved.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Homework dependence is an access penalty for students without reliable home devices.\"},{\"label\":\"In class on school devices, with the low-tech path as the default fallback.\",\"next\":\"end_strong\",\"feedback\":\"This removes the home-access penalty and makes the tool optional rather than load-bearing.\"}]},\"end_weak\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the lesson depended on the tool, or the fallback was lesser, so some students were excluded or short-changed.\",\"feedback\":\"A defensible memo never lets the AI tool be the single point of failure for learning.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_strong\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the AI tool is optional, the fallback is equivalent and in-class, and the lesson survives the outage and the opt-out.\",\"feedback\":\"This memo degrades gracefully: the learning target holds even when the technology is gone.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why an equivalent low-tech fallback must reach the same learning target rather than a simpler version of it, even though a simpler task would still give opted-out students something to do.\" answer=\"The learning target, not the tool, is what the teacher is accountable for. A simpler fallback fixes the access problem on paper while creating a quality gap: opted-out and no-device students would be assessed against a lower standard, which is its own inequity. An equivalent fallback (a printed peer-review protocol and a teacher conference that reach the same persuasive-writing target) ensures every student moves toward the same goal regardless of whether they used the tool, so the fallback is a real route, not a lesser one for 'those kids.'\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen writing an AI policy memo, run the privacy and consent checks first, the equity-of-logistics check second, and the learning-purpose-plus-fallback check third. Before you treat the memo as finished, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Data check:** What happens to student data, stated explicitly rather than assumed?\n2. **Access check:** What is the real path for a student who opts out or lacks a device, and does it reach the same target?\n3. **Failure check:** If the tool is unavailable tomorrow, does the lesson still work for everyone?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\n<inline-check question=\"A memo names a fallback but assigns the AI tool as homework. Which policy check does this still fail?\" explanation=\"The equity-of-logistics check. Students without reliable home devices cannot use a tool assigned as homework, so homework dependence is an access penalty even when a fallback exists. Required AI use should stay in class on school devices.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The privacy check, because data handling is unstated.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The equity-of-logistics check, because home-device dependence penalizes some students.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"None; naming any fallback satisfies all checks.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The consent check, because homework cannot be opted out of.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nA frequent error is treating the fallback as a courtesy rather than a requirement, something to improvise if a student complains. By then the exclusion has already happened. A second error is offering a fallback that reaches a lower target, which fixes the access problem on paper while creating a quality gap in practice; the fallback must be equivalent, not lesser. A third is treating privacy as a checkbox and not reading the vendor terms, which is where retention-for-training clauses usually hide.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose you are writing the memo for an AI math-practice tool. Begin with data: does the vendor store student responses, and for how long? Write the answer in the memo. Then write the opt-out path and confirm it reaches the same skill, perhaps a printed problem set with the same targeted practice. Move the required use into class on school devices so no student is penalized at home. Finally, state the failure plan: if the platform is down, the printed set runs the lesson without interruption. That sentence is what makes the tool optional rather than load-bearing.\n\nThe artifact is the memo with explicit privacy and consent language, an equivalent opt-out path, in-class equity protection, and a named fallback that survives the tool being gone.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a policy memo by asking what breaks if the tool disappears. If the answer is \"the lesson,\" the fallback is missing. Check that data handling is stated, not assumed, and that the opt-out path reaches the same target rather than a reduced one. Keep a two-column note: each of the four checks on the left, the memo sentence that satisfies it on the right. A strong memo has a sentence for every check and a fallback that any student could take without disadvantage.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA policy memo meets the standard when a mentor teacher can see explicit data handling, a real consent path, in-class equity protection, and an equivalent fallback. Formatting is the last layer; the four checks and the fallback are load-bearing. If a check has no sentence, annotate the gap and ask BigAL to compare your memo against an exemplar that names a fallback rather than to make the language sound more official. A useful closing line reads: \"I made the AI tool optional and named an equivalent low-tech path because the learning target cannot depend on the tool, and I rejected the all-students-must-use version because it excluded opted-out and no-device students by omission.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the memo to a tool used for a high-stakes summative assessment rather than for practice. The fallback requirement intensifies: if any student might be assessed without the tool, the non-AI path must be demonstrably equivalent in difficulty and standard, or the assessment is unfair. Rewriting the memo for the summative case, with attention to comparability of the two paths, rather than reusing the practice-tool version, shows you can scale the fallback principle to higher stakes.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-06-low-tech-fallbacks-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks for CAT 531 08.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the memo states data handling explicitly, gives an equivalent opt-out path, keeps required use in class on school devices, and names a fallback that works when the tool is gone.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "stem": "A policy memo says: \"All students will use the AI writing coach for the persuasive essay unit.\" Why is a low-tech fallback needed?", "options": ["Because the AI coach is too expensive.", "Because the memo makes the tool a single point of failure: if it is down, consent is declined, or a device fails, learning stops.", "Because students dislike writing essays.", "Because the unit should not include persuasion."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible memo names a fallback so the AI tool is never the only path to the learning target.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_tool_mandatory", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531", "cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "The memo says nothing about families who decline AI use. What problem does this silence create?", "options": ["It makes the memo shorter.", "It makes the AI tool mandatory by omission, since declining families are left with no equivalent path.", "It is fine because most families agree.", "It means the unit cannot be graded."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Without a stated opt-out and an equivalent route, the tool becomes required by default for everyone.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_consent_by_omission", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "Which arrangement best protects students without reliable home devices while still allowing AI use?", "options": ["Assign the AI coach as nightly homework for everyone.", "Keep all AI use in class on school devices, never as required homework, with a printed peer-review fallback for opted-out students.", "Tell students to use a library if they have no device.", "Drop the unit for students without devices."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "In-class use on school devices plus a non-AI path keeps home access from becoming a penalty.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_homework_access_gap", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_low_tech_fallbacks_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A reviewer asks what happens to student essays the AI coach processes. Which memo line answers the privacy check?", "options": ["The tool is popular with teachers.", "The vendor contract bars retention of student text for model training.", "Students should avoid writing anything personal.", "The essays are graded quickly."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Stating the data-retention boundary answers the privacy check a reviewer would raise.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_silent_on_privacy", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_low_tech_fallbacks", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "0389829961472d3e37e870a40d4d0d5915db0b46851ce7aaaab8fcf40c4029e3", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_tool_mandatory", "pattern": "making_the_ai_tool_the_single_point_of_failure", "description": "Writing a policy that requires every student to use the AI tool, so learning stops when the tool, consent, or device is unavailable", "trigger": "memo mandates the tool with no equivalent non-AI path", "feedback": "Name a low-tech fallback that reaches the same learning target so no student's learning depends on the tool.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_consent_by_omission", "pattern": "making_ai_mandatory_by_omitting_consent", "description": "Leaving consent out of the memo so families who decline have no path and the AI tool becomes mandatory by default", "trigger": "memo is silent on opt-out and gives declining families no route", "feedback": "State the opt-out path. Families that decline still need an equivalent route to the same target.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_homework_access_gap", "pattern": "ignoring_the_home_device_access_gap", "description": "Requiring AI tool use as homework, which penalizes students without reliable home devices or internet", "trigger": "memo assigns AI tool use outside school with no access note", "feedback": "Keep AI use in class on school devices, or provide an offline path, so home access is not a penalty.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_6_fallback_silent_on_privacy", "pattern": "silent_on_student_data_privacy", "description": "Omitting whether the vendor retains student work for model training, leaving a privacy risk unaddressed", "trigger": "memo names no data-retention boundary for student text", "feedback": "State the privacy boundary: confirm student text is not retained by the vendor for training.", "rail_action